# What moving the sidebar stones onto the lane's own grid changes.
#
#   gate_diff.py --expect tools/porcelain_gate/expect/sidebar-tab-grid.expect \
#                <baseline>/<lane>:<outdir>/<lane> ...
#
# Baseline: the commit this branch left (e1ab6b493), captured with the same
# harness and with binaries built from this worktree. Do NOT concatenate this
# with gameframe-layout.expect, chat-housing.expect or any of the others: each
# of those declares what a DIFFERENT change did against a different baseline,
# and every one of their lines is stale against this one -- which the
# comparator fails on, correctly.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The defect.
#
# Classic Fixed laid its fourteen stones on a table read off its own art. The
# two bands it blits, `backhmid1` and `backbase2`, carry the 2004 frame's
# hollows, and the 2004 frame's tabs were not a grid: measured off the sprites
# -- column luminance over the hollow rows, whose minima are the dividers --
# the top band's grooves fall at 544, 571, 600, 626, 664, 697, 725 and 752, so
# its cells are 27, 29, 26, 38, 33, 28 and 27 columns wide, the bottom band's
# are at 544, 572, 600, 626, 663, 696, 724 and 749, and the run starts at 538.
#
# rev-239 puts its stones 33 apart from 526, and 161 puts them 33 apart from
# 528. Not one tab stood on one of those cells. The leftmost stone of each row
# sat on bare rock with its hollow twelve columns to its right; the selected
# one wore the 44-column redstone the 2004 grid's single wide cell was cut for,
# eleven columns wider than the tab, bleeding into both its neighbours; and
# tabs two and five were visibly off-centre in the hollows behind them.
#
# The fix takes each stone's box from the tab itself -- an element of kind TAB
# resolved through the profile's `[tabs]` map, the way the chat bar already
# takes each filter's -- and re-cuts the art to it: one hollow out of each
# band, three-sliced sideways and laid at the seven boxes that row reports, and
# the lit redstone cut the same way. Nothing here reads a lane's name; the
# guard on adopting the grid is that the seven boxes are one size and fall
# inside the band this layout has already blitted, which is why 601's two
# columns of stones down the window edge do not take it.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The pictures land in different image slots.
#
# The two re-cut bands and the re-cut stone are pictures the frame did not
# have before, so everything allocated after them renumbers -- the four orbs
# and the two band pieces report a different `scene` at exactly the same box.
#
# THE CLAIM, stated so it can be argued with: a slot id is an internal handle
# and not behaviour. Nothing reads it, nothing persists it, and no two builds
# are obliged to agree on it. It is the same line gameframe-layout.expect
# carries, for the same reason.
normalise scene = the image slot is an internal handle, and a composed picture the frame did not have before renumbers what is allocated after it

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. The boxes the stones LEAVE: the 2004 strip's own cells.
#
# The boxes over the four lanes that run Classic Fixed: classic548,
# classic161, remount164 which starts on it, and remount548 which ends on it.
# They are the cell table's fourteen rows and the icon origins beside them, at
# five widths -- 44, 38, 34, 33 and 30 -- which is the tell that they were read
# off a picture rather than off a tab.
#
# Matched by the box and size rather than by the whole capture line, because
# the three controls a stone has (its hit box, its face, its icon) write the
# same line but for that box, and a stone whose three agree is the thing this
# change produces. A box declared here that no capture has is reported STALE,
# so the list cannot be padded.
only-before abs=538,170 38x36 wh= = a stone at the 2004 strip's own cell, which is not where any tab of either grid stands
only-before abs=538,170 34x36 wh= = a stone at the 2004 strip's own cell, which is not where any tab of either grid stands
